---
import CaseStudy from '../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';
import Claim from '../../components/Claim.astro';
import ConfusionGrid from '../../components/ConfusionGrid.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import { papers } from '../../data/content';
import type { ClaimId } from '../../data/claims';

const sources: ClaimId[] = [
  'iia.scenarios', 'iia.tp', 'iia.fp', 'iia.fn', 'iia.tn', 'iia.mcc', 'iia.precision', 'iia.recall',
  'iia.base.tp', 'iia.base.fp', 'iia.base.fn', 'iia.base.tn', 'ae.tests',
];
const toc = [
  { id: 'failure', label: 'The failure' },
  { id: 'checks', label: 'What it checks' },
  { id: 'results', label: 'Results' },
  { id: 'errors', label: 'The errors' },
  { id: 'origin', label: 'Where it came from' },
  { id: 'reproduce', label: 'Reproduce' },
];

const late = `def dispatch(plan):
    execute(plan)                  # the guarded operation runs first
    if plan.flagged:
        validate_plan(plan)        # the gate is in the call set, too late`;
---
<CaseStudy
  title="Implementation Integrity Analyzer"
  dek="Static analysis for safety checks that AI-generated code defines but never runs."
  description="A deterministic static analyzer for silent implementation integrity failures in AI-generated Python: safety checks that are written but never called."
  year="2026"
  stack={['Python', 'AST analysis', 'pytest', 'GitHub Actions']}
  links={[
    { label: 'Preprint', href: papers.iia.pdf },
    { label: 'Project page', href: papers.iia.page },
    { label: 'Code', href: papers.iia.code },
  ]}
  sources={sources}
  toc={toc}
  ogImage="/og/integrity-analyzer.png"
>
  <h2 id="failure">The failure</h2>
  <p>
    An AI coding agent reports that it has wired a safety check into an execution path. The code parses, imports and passes its
    tests, and the check never runs. The function exists, nothing calls it, and no error is raised.
  </p>
  <p>
    I ran into this while building AegisOps with a coding assistant, which twice scaffolded the safety gate without wiring it
    in. Reviewing the diffs for architecture, not just syntax, is what caught it. This analyzer is an attempt to make that review
    mechanical.
  </p>

  <h2 id="checks">What it checks</h2>
  <p>It looks for three signatures in Python source.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Functions whose bodies contain only scaffold markers.</li>
    <li>Stated intent that matches no identifier in the implementation.</li>
    <li>Safety-critical functions that are defined but never called from the operations they are meant to guard.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    It is deterministic, and it does not discover which functions are safety-critical. Someone has to say so, and a wrong
    configuration produces confident wrong answers.
  </p>

  <h2 id="results">Results</h2>
  <p>
    The benchmark has <Claim id="iia.scenarios" /> scenarios: five files with one seeded integrity failure each, five correctly
    wired files that must not be flagged, and five hard cases built to sit outside what intra-file analysis can see.
  </p>
  <ConfusionGrid />
  <p>
    MCC <Claim id="iia.mcc" />, precision <Claim id="iia.precision" />, recall <Claim id="iia.recall" />. MCC is the headline
    because the corpus is small and imbalanced. You can check it from the four cells:
  </p>
  <math display="block" aria-label="MCC equals 5 times 5 minus 4 times 1, over the square root of 9 times 6 times 9 times 6, which is 21 over 54, or 0.389">
    <mrow>
      <mi>MCC</mi><mo>=</mo>
      <mfrac>
        <mrow><mn class="m-good">5</mn><mo>·</mo><mn class="m-good">5</mn><mo>−</mo><mn class="m-bad">4</mn><mo>·</mo><mn class="m-bad">1</mn></mrow>
        <msqrt><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo>·</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>·</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>·</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msqrt>
      </mfrac>
      <mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>21</mn><mn>54</mn></mfrac><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.389</mn>
    </mrow>
  </math>
  <p>
    The comparison is a string-presence check that asks whether the gate's name appears in the file. It never flags anything:
    <Claim id="iia.base.tp" /> caught, <Claim id="iia.base.fp" /> false alarms, <Claim id="iia.base.fn" /> missed, <Claim id="iia.base.tn" /> left alone,
    because a function's name always appears in its own definition. With nothing flagged, precision is 0/0 and one factor in MCC's
    denominator is zero, so both are undefined. A text search cannot tell a gate that exists from a gate that runs.
  </p>

  <h2 id="errors">The errors</h2>
  <p>
    The analyzer gets all ten straightforward cases right. Its <Claim id="iia.fp" /> false positives and <Claim id="iia.fn" /> false negative all come from the hard cases, and each one matches a limitation already documented in the
    code: no alias resolution, no calls through helper functions, no awareness of abstract methods, and no ordering analysis.
  </p>
  <p>
    The false negative matters most. The gate is called, but in a branch that does not run before the guarded operation.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={late} label="the shape of the miss" del={[2]} hot={[4]} />
  <p>
    <span class="illustrative">Illustrative, not the benchmark file.</span>{' '}
    The analyzer asks whether the gate's name is in the operation's call set. It is, so the file is reported clean. Being in a call
    set is not the same as running before the action, and no threshold fixes that. It is the one error in the direction that
    ships an unguarded operation.
  </p>
  <p>
    Nothing was excluded, relabelled or reweighted after seeing the results. The benchmark is small and I wrote it myself, so
    these numbers describe one detector on one corpus. They say nothing about how common the failure is in the wild.
  </p>

  <h2 id="origin">Where it came from</h2>
  <p>
    The analyzer lives inside the AegisOps repository and is moving to its own. The results above were produced at a commit where
    <Claim id="ae.tests" /> AegisOps tests pass. At that point AegisOps asked a language model to propose the whole allocation plan.
    It has since been rebuilt so that a solver plans and the model only reads and drafts. The <a href="/work/aegisops/">AegisOps case study</a> covers
    that change and why it happened.
  </p>

  <h2 id="reproduce">Reproduce</h2>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" label="terminal" code={`git clone https://github.com/Avnish1505/aegisops-ai
cd aegisops-ai
pip install -r requirements-dev.txt
pytest`} />
</CaseStudy>

<style>
  :global(.m-good) {
    color: var(--mint);
  }
  :global(.m-bad) {
    color: var(--rose);
  }
</style>
